clean_summary:
GPT-3 was a sensation because it claimed language models are few-shot in-context learners. I wonder why we dropped the ball on in-context learning and moved to mostly execution-oriented research on LLMs, focusing on training for any high-value task, a set that will keep increasing indefinitely.

Maybe we'll get these data centers with geniuses, but they are only geniuses on tasks that your favorite frontier lab decides to directly or indirectly optimize for.

@deliprao: "In-context learning in LLMs."

Very well written blog. Rishabh Agarwal thinks of reinforcement learning as learning from interventions, which helps explain why it can be a more powerful paradigm than supervised learning.

He suggests that learning from counterfactuals—what could have happened under different actions—is an area where RL could improve, especially when combined with world modeling.

@vishalmisra: "@ShriramKMurthi @Hesamation Have you read this"

Optimistic about unhobbling large language models if issues like hallucination from burning feedback into weights and reasoning verbalization collapse can be addressed with naive self-distillation.

@rish2k1: "A dense training signal that points in the wrong direction."

The linked article explains why on-policy distillation works and why naive self-distillation often fails, highlighting the challenge of misleading training signals in model improvement efforts.
